---
import BrandMark from "./BrandMark.astro";
import Card from "./Card/index.astro";
import {WELCOME} from "../content/home";
import {markFill} from "../lib/brand-mark";
import {PATCHES} from "../content/races";
import {LINKS, METADATA} from "../content/site";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";
import EventsLink from "./EventsLink.astro";
import ThemeSwitcher from "./ThemeSwitcher.astro";
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import portfolioImage from '../assets/me.webp'

/*
 * The first line of the stack is the greeting and carries the mark; the rest are
 * taglines. Split here rather than by index inside the markup, because only the
 * first one shares its row with the theme control.
 */
const [greeting, ...taglines] = WELCOME.description

/*
 * THE MARK IS 1.4x THE TEXT, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN TASTED. At `text-xl` a mark
 * drawn at 1em puts each of the sunrise's five rays under 2px wide and they close up against
 * the dome — the mark reads as a filled half-disc and the thing that makes it this mark is
 * gone. 1.4em is where the fan survives in a run of text. It is chosen against a rendered
 * ladder, and the cost is a slightly taller line box, which `<main>`'s height budget was
 * measured against rather than assumed.
 *
 * The name prints the figure and says what it is a figure OF. That is the Data Visualization
 * obligation discharged in the one placement that has room for it; see `WELCOME.mark_label`.
 */
const markLabel = WELCOME.mark_label.replace("{percent}", String(Math.round(markFill() * 100)))

/*
 * THE QUIET CONTROLS ARE LAID OUT FOUR TO A ROW, AND THIS IS A GROUPING RATHER THAN A
 * COLUMN COUNT. The difference is the whole reason it is allowed to exist.
 *
 * What `uno.config.ts` records at length, and what `tests/control-geometry.test.ts`
 * gates, is that no rule may GRANT this row a number of columns at a width. Three media
 * queries once did — 3, 2 and 1 columns — and a hand-tuned count compared against a
 * text-relative box has to be re-tuned whenever either side moves. It was wrong twice, in
 * opposite directions, and the second time the card sheared 136.84px of hero copy at 320px
 * and a 40px root.
 *
 * This is not that. Nothing here reads a width: the items are chunked into rows of four
 * ONCE, at every viewport and every text size, and each row is still an ordinary wrapping
 * flex row whose minimum content width is one box. Enlarge the reader's text and each row
 * reflows on its own exactly as the single row did — the four-up reading degrades, and
 * nothing clips. So the invariant the gate protects is untouched; what is authored is
 * which items sit together, which is a fact about the ITEMS.
 *
 * THE PREFERENCE TAKES THE LAST CELL. It is appended to the final row when that row has
 * room and takes a row of its own when it does not, so it is always last however many
 * destinations there are. Nothing in the drawing marks it out and nothing needs to: every
 * destination is a solid brand mark and this is a stroked UI mark, it is a `button` where
 * they are anchors, and the design system's own words for this box are "a member of a set,
 * where the marks are the vocabulary, and for a preference".
 */
const PER_ROW = 4
const linkRows = Array.from(
    {length: Math.ceil(LINKS.length / PER_ROW)},
    (_, i) => LINKS.slice(i * PER_ROW, (i + 1) * PER_ROW),
)
const lastRowIsFull = linkRows[linkRows.length - 1].length >= PER_ROW

/*
 * Mobile hero (below `md`), see the <style> rules at the foot of this file.
 *
 * The portrait bleeds off the card's right edge behind the type. Below `md` it
 * used to be painted at 70% opacity as a flat rectangle, which put mid-grey
 * photo pixels under 20px/300 type: 2.75:1 at worst, i.e. unreadable, and it
 * washed the face out at the same time.
 *
 * The fix separates the two jobs that one opacity value was doing badly at once:
 *
 *   1. `.portrait`'s mask feathers the three edges that fall *inside* the card
 *      (left, top, bottom) so the photo dissolves into the card instead of
 *      cutting a rectangle out of it. The right edge is deliberately not
 *      feathered: it bleeds past the card and the card's clipping handles it.
 *      With the seams gone the photo no longer needs to be knocked back, so it
 *      renders at full opacity and the face is the focal point again.
 *
 *   2. `.intro-type::after` is a scrim: it veils the photo *only* where the
 *      type sits, capping the photo's contribution behind the text at 32%.
 *      It is a pseudo-element of the copy column, not a fixed-size overlay,
 *      so it tracks that column's real width and height — add or lengthen a
 *      WELCOME.description line and the protected area grows with it.
 *
 * Legibility is therefore arithmetic over the theme tokens, not a property of
 * this particular photograph: the worst pixel any image can contain still
 * clears WCAG 1.4.3 in both themes. tests/mobile-hero-contrast.test.ts recomputes
 * that from the built stylesheet.
 *
 * `md:` and up is untouched: the mask and the scrim are both switched off and
 * the portrait goes back in flow with its pink offset shadow.
 *
 * THREE CLASSES HERE EXIST BECAUSE THE CONTROL ROW STOPPED COUNTING COLUMNS, and all
 * three are consequences of one fact: a wrapping row's MAX-content width is every
 * control on a single line (34rem) where the old column grid's was four of them
 * (19rem). The row's minimum shrank, which is the point — but its maximum grew, and
 * this card's copy column is what asks for it. Each was measured before and after.
 *
 *   1. `shrink-0` ON THE PORTRAIT'S WRAPPER. The copy column and the portrait share a
 *      `justify-between` row, so a copy column asking for 34rem takes the difference
 *      out of the photo: the portrait shrank 275 -> 203.48 at `md` and at 1024, i.e.
 *      26%, and by 13-26% at 104 of the 113 widths from 768 to 2560. Pinning the
 *      wrapper makes the copy column absorb the deficit instead, and the portrait
 *      measures 275.00 at every configuration again while the row still wraps to more
 *      controls per line than the ladder allowed.
 *
 *   2. `self-start md:self-auto` ON `.intro-type`. The scrim is a pseudo-element of
 *      the type column, so it is only "over the type" while that column is as wide
 *      as the type. Stretched to the control row's width it dragged across the
 *      portrait — 97.55% of the photo's on-screen width veiled at 360-640 against 81.63% and
 *      0% before — which is the mask's job undone and the photo's focal point with it.
 *      Not stretching below `md` restores the earlier figures exactly.
 *
 *   3. `max-h-full md:max-h-[415px]` ON THE PORTRAIT. Wrapping makes this card
 *      SHORTER, and below `md` the portrait is positioned against the card, so an
 *      absolute cap the card can now undercut leaves the photo overhanging: at 658-767
 *      the card came out 246px against the portrait's 275, and the 15.5px cut off each
 *      end truncated the vertical mask feather, so the photo met the card's inner
 *      edges as a hard rectangle — the exact seam the mask exists to dissolve. Capping
 *      against the card keeps the feather intact; `md:` restores the absolute cap for
 *      the in-flow portrait, which is bounded by its own row instead.
 */
---

<Card colSpan="md:col-span-2 lg:col-span-3" rowSpan="md:row-span-4" fillsArea>
    <div class="flex w-full h-full justify-between md:pr-8">
        {/*
            THE COPY COLUMN IS CENTRED, AND IT USED TO PUSH ITS CONTROLS TO THE FOOT. Recording the
            swap, because the arrangement it replaces was deliberate and its reason expired rather
            than being overridden.

            At `md` and up this column is a STRETCHED flex item under a cap, so its height is that
            cap whatever it holds — it does not size to its content and never did. While the
            controls were seven plated boxes the content nearly filled it, and pinning them to the
            foot with `justify-between` plus a stretching type column was what kept the slack out of
            the middle. Retiring the plate took the content from 300px to 224, so the same
            arrangement put all 76px of the difference in ONE place: a 92px hole between the last
            tagline and the strip, measured at every width from 1024 up. That is the arrangement
            working exactly as written, on content it was not measured against.

            Centring spends the same slack as 38px above and 38px below, which reads as the card's
            own rhythm rather than as a hole, and it puts this column's optical centre within 3.5px
            of the portrait's — the portrait is centred in its own box, so the two columns now
            agree. Below `md` there is no slack to spend and this is an exact no-op.

            DO NOT PUT THE SLACK BACK AT ONE END. Both ends were built and measured: at the foot it
            is the 92px hole above, and at the top it is 76px of empty card below the call to
            action, which is the failure this file's own history calls a 130px hole.
        */}
        <div class="flex flex-col justify-center md:max-h-[18.75rem] gap-4">
            <div class="intro-type flex flex-col self-start md:self-auto">
                {/*
                    ONE TOP-LEVEL HEADING, AND NO EYEBROW. Both halves are the same judgement and both are
                    scored against the frontend-design skill rather than asserted:

                    "welcome" was a level-6 heading — it opened the document's outline at level
                    six, one line above the level-one heading saying "Hi, I'm Calvin". The skill's STRUCTURE IS
                    INFORMATION test asks whether a structural device encodes something true
                    about the content: a heading level is a claim about depth, and level 6
                    above level 1 is a false one. Its writing section asks that each element
                    do exactly ONE job, and the word did none the greeting does not already
                    do — which is the maintainer's own habit of deleting copy the heading
                    implies. Chanel's REMOVE ONE ACCESSORY names it as the accessory: it is
                    the only ornament in the hero and the only string on the page not under
                    `src/content/`. Provenance settles the last doubt — `git log -S welcome`
                    traces it to the upstream template author, not to a decision taken here.

                    The two taglines were top-level headings too, so the page shipped THREE. They are not
                    headings of anything: nothing is sectioned under them, and a reader
                    navigating by heading got three top-level entries before the first real
                    one. They keep every class they wore, so THAT half draws nothing new —
                    the change is what the markup CLAIMS, not what it paints.

                    BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT MOVED, because the two halves differ. Measured on
                    real builds either side: at 1440x900 the card stays 824x357 and the
                    document stays 900, so desktop is untouched. On a 390px phone the
                    document goes 1774 -> 1754 — the eyebrow's own line, given back. Deleting
                    a line of copy is not a no-op and should not be described as one.
                */}
                {/*
                    THE GREETING CARRIES NO CONTROL, AND IT DID FOR ONE REVISION, so the reason it
                    does not is recorded rather than left to be rediscovered.

                    The theme control sat here, hard right, on the argument that this line is a
                    masthead. It could not hold the position: at `md` and up this column stretches
                    and the control landed on the copy column's right rail, but BELOW `md` this
                    container is `self-start` — load-bearing for the mobile hero, since stretching
                    it drags the scrim across the portrait, 97.55% of the photo veiled against
                    81.63% — so the row is only as wide as the widest tagline and the control came
                    to rest 74.5px short of the rail everything else on the card shares. One
                    control, two arrangements, and on a phone it was aligned to a rail nothing else
                    used. It also made this line 44px tall against the taglines' 30.

                    The preference is a quiet control and it now sits with the other quiet controls,
                    which is where a reader looks for it and where the vocabulary already puts it.
                */}
                <h1 class="m-0 font-light text-xl"><BrandMark size="1.4em" label={markLabel}/> {greeting}</h1>
                {taglines.map((item) => (<p class="m-0 font-light text-xl">{item}</p>))}
            </div>
            {/*
                WHERE TO FIND ME, DRAWN QUIETLY, AND THEN THE ONE WAY IN.

                These used to be the site's loudest mark, seven times over on one screen: the
                profiles, the résumé and the theme control, every one of them plated. The plate
                is the mark for a card's ONE action, so nine wearers on the home page was that
                mark spent on furniture — and it inverted the hierarchy, drawing the ways to
                LEAVE louder than the one way further in. They are members of a set, which is
                what the quiet glyph box is for.

                THE BLOCK'S WIDTH IS WHAT SETS THIS COLUMN'S, AND THEREFORE THE CALL TO ACTION'S.
                A copy column takes the widest max-content among its children, and four boxes at
                this separation ask for 200px against the taglines' 229.52 — so the TYPE wins,
                the column is the width of its longest line, and the call to action below is that
                width too. One rail for the whole column, and it moves only when the copy does.
                Measured at 1024, 1440, 430 and 393; the room available is 339, 435, 332 and 295,
                so only the narrowest of those clamps it.

                THE ORDER IS THE DECISION AND IT IS ALSO THE FOCUS ORDER. Identity, then where to
                find me, then the way in — so the card ends on the way FURTHER INTO the site
                rather than on the ways out of it, and its action sits at the foot exactly as each
                goal card's does. The accepted cost is that a keyboard reader meets the
                destinations before the card's own action; the DOM sequence and the visual
                sequence must stay identical (SC 1.3.2, 2.4.3), so do not reorder either in CSS.
            */}
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
                {linkRows.map((row, index) => (
                        <div class="link-strip">
                            {row.map(({link, logo, name}) => (<a href={link} target="_blank" class="chip-icon">
                                <span class={`${iconClass(logo)} shrink-0`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                                <span class="sr-only">{name}</span>
                            </a>))}
                            {!lastRowIsFull && index === linkRows.length - 1 && <ThemeSwitcher/>}
                        </div>
                ))}
                {lastRowIsFull && (
                        <div class="link-strip">
                            <ThemeSwitcher/>
                        </div>
                )}
            </div>
            {/*
                THE WHOLE WALL HAD NO DOOR FROM THIS PAGE until this link existed, and it opened
                as a run of underlined words — the quietest idiom the site has, on the way into
                its signature content, while six outbound profiles beside it wore the loudest.
                It is the intro card's one action now and it is drawn as one, which is the same
                object each goal card already ends on.

                IT IS THE SAME COMPONENT AS THOSE TWO, deliberately: the label wrapping, the
                keyboard focus indicator measured against the plate, and the forced-colours repair are all
                properties of a control rather than of a card, and a second call to action would
                own three copies of them. Its label is `PATCHES.heading` — the very words the
                page it opens is headed with, which `tests/build-output.test.ts` asserts in both
                directions — and its mark is the mirror of the site's own way back.
            */}
            <EventsLink href="/patches" label={PATCHES.heading}/>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center p-4 shrink-0">
            <Image
                    src={portfolioImage}
                    alt={`${METADATA.name}`}
                    width="275"
                    height="275"
                    densities={[2]}
                    format="webp"
                    class="portrait w-auto max-h-full md:max-h-[415px] select-none absolute right-[-72px] top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 z-[-2] md:relative md:right-auto md:top-auto md:transform-none md:z-auto pointer-events-none md:shadow-[10px_10px_0_var(--shadow)] rounded-[8px]"
                    loading="eager"
            />
        </div>
    </div>
</Card>

<style>
    /* Feathered edges, so the portrait dissolves into the card.
       No -webkit- twins: this project's minifier de-duplicates prefixed
       declarations against their unprefixed originals and drops them, so
       authoring them shipped nothing. An engine without CSS masking simply
       gets no mask — the seams return, but the type stays readable, because
       contrast is the scrim's job and the scrim does not depend on this. */
    .portrait {
        mask-image:
                linear-gradient(to right, transparent 0, #000 100px),
                linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0, #000 56px, #000 calc(100% - 56px), transparent 100%);
        /* Intersect, so an edge is feathered if *either* gradient dims it. */
        mask-composite: intersect;
    }

    .intro-type {
        position: relative; /* containing box for the scrim; NOT a stacking
                               context (z-index stays auto), so the scrim's
                               z-index still resolves against the card. */
    }

    /* The scrim. 68% of the page colour caps the portrait's contribution behind
       the type at 32%, which holds 4.5:1 for *any* photo in either theme — the
       worst case is pure black under #0B0B0B text and pure white under #FAFAFA
       text, i.e. a value derived from the theme tokens rather than tuned to
       today's pixels. It paints one layer above the portrait.

       Both soft edges are cut by the MASK, not by the paint. That matters twice:
       a background-image cannot be transitioned, so a gradient fill snapped to
       the new theme while the card behind it crossfaded — a hard-edged panel
       flashing over the copy on every theme toggle; and forced-colors forces
       background-image off, which would have squared the right edge off there
       too. As a flat background-color it crossfades with the card and the ramp
       survives every mode. */
    .intro-type::after {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        z-index: -1;
        pointer-events: none;
        top: -24px; /* the card's padding: reach its inner edges */
        left: -24px;
        bottom: -56px; /* room for the downward fade to finish below the type */
        width: calc(100% + 76px); /* 24px left bleed + 52px right ramp */
        background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--background) 68%, transparent);
        transition: background-color 0.3s ease; /* match the card's crossfade */
        mask-image:
                linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 calc(100% - 40px), transparent 100%),
                linear-gradient(to right, #000 calc(100% - 44px), transparent 100%);
        mask-composite: intersect;
    }

    /* No color-mix() means the declaration above is dropped whole, which would
       leave the type sitting on an unveiled photo. Fall back to an *opaque*
       band of the card's own colour: the photo stops showing through behind the
       type on those browsers, but the type lands on the same surface as every
       other word on the site. Legibility never depends on color-mix support. */
    @supports not (background-color: color-mix(in srgb, #000 50%, transparent)) {
        .intro-type::after {
            background-color: var(--card-background);
        }
    }

    /* Forced-colors mode: images are exempt from colour forcing, so without an
       opaque surface the type would land straight on the photograph. Repaint it
       so forced CanvasText sits on forced Canvas. The mask still applies here,
       so the right edge stays soft rather than cutting through the portrait. */
    @media (forced-colors: active) {
        .intro-type::after {
            background-color: Canvas;
        }
    }

    /* Mirrors UnoCSS's `md` breakpoint: desktop keeps the in-flow portrait. That
       breakpoint is text-relative, so this one is too — 48rem is the same 768 CSS
       pixels at the default root size, and it moves with the reader's text where an
       absolute bound would not. If these two ever part company the mobile hero's
       mask and scrim outlive the one-column layout they exist for. */
    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
        .portrait {
            mask-image: none;
        }

        .intro-type {
            /* Not merely cosmetic: a positioned element paints in a later phase
               than an in-flow one. Where the md grid squeezes this card the
               last tagline line already overlaps the button row, and leaving
               this positioned flips which of the two paints on top. */
            position: static;
        }

        .intro-type::after {
            display: none;
        }
    }

    /*
     * The six social glyphs and the hero's way to the wall are icon masks, repainted for
     * forced colours by the shared rule in BasicLayout.astro — which carries the mechanism,
     * the measurement and the reason it is scoped to masks rather than to every decorative
     * span. Both cases were erased before it existed: the six painted rgb(255,255,255) on a
     * card of rgb(255,255,255), 1.00:1, and 784 of 784 pixels of the link's arrow were Canvas.
     *
     * They are DIFFERENT FAULTS and it is worth saying so. An icon-only control loses its
     * NAME when its glyph goes — the row degraded to six identical empty boxes with no way to
     * tell GitHub from LinkedIn from the resume. The text link keeps its words and its
     * underline, and loses a signifier it did not need twice; its mark is repaired because it
     * is meant to be there, not because the link stops working without it.
     */
</style>
